  ('f29ee0de-73ec-518f-a4bd-63f29783c2d8', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'math-g9-linear-and-exponential-models'), null, 'easy',
   $q$A table gives these values: when $x = 0, 1, 2, 3$, the values of $y$ are $5, 8, 11, 14$. Which statement describes the relationship?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Linear, because $y$ increases by $3$ each time $x$ increases by $1$","is_correct":true},{"text":"Exponential, because $y$ increases each time $x$ increases by $1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"increasing-means-exponential"},{"text":"Exponential, because $y$ is multiplied by $3$ each time $x$ increases by $1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-difference-with-factor"},{"text":"Linear, because $y$ is multiplied by $\\frac{8}{5}$ each time $x$ increases by $1$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"checked-only-first-ratio"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Linear, because $y$ increases by $3$ each time $x$ increases by $1$$q$, $q$The differences are $8 - 5 = 3$, $11 - 8 = 3$ and $14 - 11 = 3$, all equal, so the pattern is linear. Increasing alone does not make a pattern exponential; exponential needs equal factors, and $\frac{8}{5} \ne \frac{11}{8}$.$q$, 'draft', null)
