   $q$Of 100 students, 40 own a pet and 60 do not. Of the pet owners, 24 walk to school. Of the students without a pet, 36 walk to school. Which statement about the events "owns a pet" and "walks to school" is correct?$q$, $q$[{"text":"They are independent, because $P(\\text{walks} \\mid \\text{pet}) = P(\\text{walks}) = 0.6$","is_correct":true},{"text":"They are not independent, because $P(\\text{walks}) \\ne P(\\text{pet})$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-the-two-marginals"},{"text":"They are not independent, because 24 is not equal to 36","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-raw-counts"},{"text":"They are not independent, because some students both walk and own a pet","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"confused-independent-with-mutually-exclusive"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$They are independent, because $P(\text{walks} \mid \text{pet}) = P(\text{walks}) = 0.6$$q$, $q$$P(\text{walks}) = \frac{60}{100} = 0.6$ and $P(\text{walks} \mid \text{pet}) = \frac{24}{40} = 0.6$, so knowing a student owns a pet does not change the probability, and the events are independent. Comparing the raw counts 24 and 36 ignores that the two groups have different sizes.$q$, 'draft', null)
