   $q$At a farmers market, 250 shoppers were surveyed. Of the 100 who bought honey, 40 also bought bread. Of the 150 who did not buy honey, 75 bought bread. Which statement correctly decides whether buying honey and buying bread are independent?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$P(\\text{bread}) = 0.46$, which is less than $0.5$, so the events are independent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-the-condition"},{"text":"$P(\\text{bread} \\mid \\text{honey}) = 0.4$ but $P(\\text{bread}) = 0.46$, so the events are not independent","is_correct":true},{"text":"$P(\\text{bread} \\mid \\text{honey}) = 0.4$ and $P(\\text{honey}) = 0.4$, so the events are independent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-to-wrong-marginal"},{"text":"$P(\\text{bread and honey}) = 0.16$, which is not 0, so the events are independent","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-nonzero-overlap-test"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$P(\text{bread} \mid \text{honey}) = 0.4$ but $P(\text{bread}) = 0.46$, so the events are not independent$q$, $q$$P(\text{bread}) = \frac{40 + 75}{250} = 0.46$, while $P(\text{bread} \mid \text{honey}) = \frac{40}{100} = 0.4$; since these differ, buying honey changes the chance of buying bread, so the events are not independent. The choice that says $P(\text{honey}) = 0.4$ means independence compares with $P(\text{honey})$, but the test must compare $P(\text{bread} \mid \text{honey})$ with $P(\text{bread})$.$q$, 'draft', null)
