   $q$In the angle bisector construction, $YM = YN$ from the first arc and $MK = NK$ from the two equal arcs, and $\overline{YK}$ is shared. Which congruence shortcut proves $\triangle YMK \cong \triangle YNK$, so that ray $YK$ bisects the angle?$q$, $q$[{"text":"HL","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-right-triangles"},{"text":"SSS","is_correct":true},{"text":"AAA","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-aaa"},{"text":"SAS","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"cited-sas-without-known-angle"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$SSS$q$, $q$All three pairs of sides are known to be equal and no angles are, so SSS applies, and the matching angles $\angle MYK$ and $\angle NYK$ are congruent. SAS would need the included angle to be known equal, which is exactly what is being proved.$q$, 'draft', null)
